  "executive_briefing": "The late 2026 international tax and trade environments are defined by structured compliance execution and escalating geopolitical friction. Over 140 jurisdictions are operationalizing the OECD Pillar Two 15% global minimum tax, backed by major economies and prompting comprehensive data tracking through the OECD's 2026 Corporate Tax Statistics. Concurrently, nations like Singapore are balancing baseline statutory corporate taxes (17%) with targeted Year of Assessment 2026 rebates, while expanding digital tax oversight through progressive Peppol-based InvoiceNow mandates extending to 2031. On trade, unilateral tariff regimes face heightened legal and retaliatory headwinds, highlighted by recent US Court of International Trade rulings on IEEPA tariffs and escalating US-Canada trade tensions.",
